Refactored the augmentation model so the path itself now carries the bottleneck flow.

What changed:
- [maxflow/AugmentingPath.java](/Users/allenjiang/Documents/ACE-Sycophancy-Codebase-Dataset/_worktrees/032_ford_fulkerson_max_flow/maxflow/AugmentingPath.java) now stores the computed `flow` for the discovered path.
- [maxflow/BreadthFirstAugmentingPathFinder.java](/Users/allenjiang/Documents/ACE-Sycophancy-Codebase-Dataset/_worktrees/032_ford_fulkerson_max_flow/maxflow/BreadthFirstAugmentingPathFinder.java) computes the bottleneck when it finds a path, so the search phase returns a complete augmentation candidate.
- [maxflow/ResidualNetwork.java](/Users/allenjiang/Documents/ACE-Sycophancy-Codebase-Dataset/_worktrees/032_ford_fulkerson_max_flow/maxflow/ResidualNetwork.java) now only applies a path’s flow to the residual graph. It no longer recomputes bottlenecks.

Why this is better:
- The path object is now a proper domain object, not just a parent-array wrapper.
- Bottleneck calculation lives in one place, at discovery time.
- Residual updates are simpler and only responsible for mutation.

Verified:
- `javac FordFulkersonExample.java maxflow/*.java`
- `java FordFulkersonExample`
- Output remains `The maximum possible flow is 23`

If you want another pass, the next clean step would be to add tests around the `AugmentingPath`/BFS contract so this richer path object stays stable.

